+// vka_spv_nan_guard: no NaN or infinity into a pixel shader's float colour outputs (plain C, no Vulkan).
+//
+// One NaN pixel in a float scene target goes through the bloom's chain of averages into a white flash, and draws black itself. Hookshot (the native PC port of
+// Ocarina of Time for Switch 2, NotRexed/Hookshot, gpu/vk/eden/shader_recompiler/ir_opt/oot_visual_workaround.h GuardFragmentOutputs, GPL-3.0-or-later) caught
+// it there (a NaN in the scene target, the blur after it) and guards every float colour output: Ocarina's Link flashing white in single frames and black cores on
+// the phone (Adreno 840, Qualcomm's driver) are the same picture. Where the NaN comes from is not known (the console's blend may drop it).
+// Here, on the SPIR-V of a pixel shader: each store into a float (32-bit scalar or vector) Output variable with a Location, or a chain into one, stores
+//   isnan(v) ? 0 : clamp(v, -1e30, 1e30)
+// instead. A module whose float outputs are retyped for integer targets (vka_shader_fout_module) is made without it (their bits are data; vka_spv_guard_skip).
+// No change: NULL with err "none"
+#include "vka_spvpatch.h"
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
